00:27:22,870 --> 00:27:28,870
We need more advanced analytics, customer journey mapping, heat mapping on our site,

523
00:27:28,870 --> 00:27:31,270
and deeper social listening.

524
00:27:31,270 --> 00:27:33,670
I agree completely.

525
00:27:33,670 --> 00:27:36,790
Social listening is especially important.

526
00:27:36,790 --> 00:27:38,550
People talk openly online,

527
00:27:38,550 --> 00:27:41,870
and those conversations reveal emerging trends

528
00:27:41,870 --> 00:27:45,110
long before traditional data sources pick them up.

529
00:27:45,110 --> 00:27:47,910
If we track sentiments, keyword shifts,

530
00:27:47,910 --> 00:27:49,790
and competitor mentions,

531
00:27:49,790 --> 00:27:53,350
we can anticipate changes in customer preferences.

532
00:27:53,350 --> 00:27:57,070
Yes, and we should also monitor micro-communities.

533
00:27:57,070 --> 00:27:59,870
Customers gather in niche online groups.

534
00:27:59,870 --> 00:28:00,570
Reddit thro-

535
00:28:00,570 --> 00:28:01,470
Good morning.

536
00:28:01,470 --> 00:28:03,870
Glad we could finally sit down today.

537
00:28:03,870 --> 00:28:07,710
I know the last few weeks have been packed with campaign reviews,

538
00:28:07,710 --> 00:28:11,390
product demos, and the Q4 content planning rush,

539
00:28:11,390 --> 00:28:16,110
but carving out time for this marketing strategy discussion is long overdue.

540
00:28:16,110 --> 00:28:17,310
Absolutely.

541
00:28:17,310 --> 00:28:19,230
And good morning to you as well.

542
00:28:19,230 --> 00:28:22,830
The digital team has been running non-stop with analytics updates

543
00:28:22,830 --> 00:28:24,350
and performance reports,

544
00:28:24,350 --> 00:28:28,510
And the creative team is already asking for clarity on next quarter's direction.

545
00:28:28,510 --> 00:28:32,670
So yes, this conversation needs to happen now before we fall behind the curve.

546
00:28:32,670 --> 00:28:35,950
Exactly. Marketing is moving too fast these days.

547
00:28:36,510 --> 00:28:40,990
Algorithms shift overnight, consumer behavior is evolving every week,

548
00:28:40,990 --> 00:28:44,590
and competition is becoming more aggressive across every channel.

549
00:28:45,150 --> 00:28:48,750
That's why today, I'd like us to revisit our positioning,

550
00:28:48,750 --> 00:28:53,870
revise our content strategy, and take a hard look at what's working and what's not.

551
00:28:53,870 --> 00:28:58,430
That sounds like a plan. To get started, maybe we should analyze the current situation.

552
00:28:58,430 --> 00:29:03,230
If you look at the last three months, we saw solid traffic growth, about 18%,

553
00:29:03,230 --> 00:29:08,510
but conversion rates stayed flat. That suggests interest is growing, but the value proposition

554
00:29:08,510 --> 00:29:13,310
isn't strong enough to push people to take action. Right. And I've noticed the same thing

555
00:29:13,310 --> 00:29:18,270
across multiple touchpoints. We're attracting leads, but we're not compelling them strongly

556
00:29:18,270 --> 00:29:23,790
enough. The funnel is filling up, but too many people drop out during the middle stages.

557
00:29:23,790 --> 00:29:26,870
It's a classic awareness without engagement issue.

558
00:29:26,870 --> 00:29:30,150
Exactly. And our messaging might be part of the problem.

559
00:29:30,150 --> 00:29:35,150
If you look across our ads, social posts, landing pages and email sequences,

560
00:29:35,150 --> 00:29:37,150
the tone is not always consistent.

561
00:29:37,150 --> 00:29:40,750
Sometimes we sound premium, sometimes we sound budget friendly,

562
00:29:40,750 --> 00:29:42,870
sometimes we sound overly corporate.

563
00:29:42,870 --> 00:29:44,790
We need one unified voice.

564
00:29:44,790 --> 00:29:47,270
Yes, brand voice is crucial.

565
00:29:47,270 --> 00:29:50,150
Consumers don't trust inconsistent brands.

566
00:29:50,150 --> 00:29:51,950
They trust familiarity.

567
00:29:51,950 --> 00:29:55,710
And in marketing, familiarity is everything.

568
00:29:55,710 --> 00:30:00,030
Our messaging needs to feel aligned no matter where customers encounter us.

569
00:30:00,030 --> 00:30:05,030
TikTok, LinkedIn, YouTube, the website, or even an email newsletter.

570
00:30:05,030 --> 00:30:06,030
I agree.

571
00:30:06,030 --> 00:30:09,070
And about platforms, our performance varies dramatically.

572
00:30:09,070 --> 00:30:10,070
Across them.

573
00:30:10,070 --> 00:30:13,910
TikTok is generating reach, but almost no qualified leads.

574
00:30:13,910 --> 00:30:17,590
LinkedIn is generating high-quality leads but at a higher cost.

575
00:30:17,590 --> 00:30:21,670
YouTube ads show great retention, but watch-to-click conversion is weak.

576
00:30:21,670 --> 00:30:23,910
The ecosystem feels fragmented.

577
00:30:23,910 --> 00:30:28,630
True, but fragmentation can be managed with a strong funnel strategy.

578
00:30:28,630 --> 00:30:32,190
We need to use each platform for its natural strengths.

579
00:30:32,190 --> 00:30:37,990
TikTok for quick awareness, Instagram for storytelling, YouTube for educational depth,

580
00:30:37,990 --> 00:30:41,830
LinkedIn for B2B authority, and email for nurturing.

581
00:30:41,830 --> 00:30:45,590
It's about assigning the right job to the right platform.

582
00:30:45,590 --> 00:30:46,590
Exactly.

583
00:30:46,590 --> 00:30:50,470
And speaking of email, our open rates and click-through rates have been dropping.

584
00:30:50,470 --> 00:30:53,230
Not dramatically, but enough to be concerning.

585
00:30:53,230 --> 00:30:57,310
I think the issue is that our newsletters became too promotional.

586
00:30:57,310 --> 00:30:59,310
People don't want constant sales pitches.

587
00:30:59,310 --> 00:31:00,930
They want value.

588
00:31:00,930 --> 00:31:05,030
We need to reintroduce educational content that solves real problems.

589
00:31:05,030 --> 00:31:06,030
Yes.

590
00:31:06,030 --> 00:31:08,470
Value first, pitch later.

591
00:31:08,470 --> 00:31:13,470
That's how modern marketing works, and we need to remember something important.

592
00:31:13,470 --> 00:31:16,070
Today's consumers are incredibly smart.

593
00:31:16,070 --> 00:31:19,230
They can smell a sales tactic from a mile away.

594
00:31:19,230 --> 00:31:25,230
So our approach needs to shift from sell this product to understand this need, then show

595
00:31:25,230 --> 00:31:28,150
how our product naturally fits.

596
00:31:28,150 --> 00:31:29,750
That's how trust is built.

597
00:31:29,750 --> 00:31:30,750
Agreed.

598
00:31:30,750 --> 00:31:32,830
And trust is everything.

599
00:31:32,830 --> 00:31:35,870
Especially now when buyers are overwhelmed with choices.

600
00:31:35,870 --> 00:31:41,150
If we want loyalty, we need to create emotional resonance, not just information.

601
00:31:41,150 --> 00:31:43,710
Storytelling should play a bigger role in our campaigns.

602
00:31:43,710 --> 00:31:44,870
Absolutely.

603
00:31:44,870 --> 00:31:47,750
Stories sell more effectively than features.

604
00:31:47,750 --> 00:31:52,730
connections connect with narratives. Instead of saying our product is fast, we should tell

605
00:31:52,730 --> 00:31:57,950
a story about someone whose life got easier because of that speed. That's the difference

606
00:31:57,950 --> 00:32:01,910
between content people scroll past and content people remember.

607
00:32:01,910 --> 00:32:06,630
Exactly. And I think our creative team is talented enough to execute that, if we

608
00:32:06,630 --> 00:32:10,830
give them clearer direction. Right now, they're producing great visuals, but the

609
00:32:10,830 --> 00:32:15,410
emotional angle is missing. We need to brief them with deeper insights into customer

610
00:32:15,410 --> 00:32:18,490
pain points, not just product specs.

611
00:32:18,490 --> 00:32:21,370
Insight-driven creativity is always more impactful.

612
00:32:21,370 --> 00:32:25,330
Yes, and that brings up the next point, market research.

613
00:32:25,330 --> 00:32:28,130
We have some data, but not enough.

614
00:32:28,130 --> 00:32:30,410
Our personas are too surface level.

615
00:32:30,410 --> 00:32:35,290
We know demographics and job titles, but we don't know beliefs, motivations, fears,

616
00:32:35,290 --> 00:32:37,170
or decision triggers.

617
00:32:37,170 --> 00:32:40,810
Without psychological insight, marketing becomes guesswork.

618
00:32:40,810 --> 00:32:41,810
Exactly.

619
00:32:41,810 --> 00:32:43,410
And guesswork is expensive.

620
00:32:43,410 --> 00:32:47,410
We need a richer understanding of what truly influences our buyers.

621
00:32:47,410 --> 00:32:50,850
For example, why do some leads hesitate to book a demo?

622
00:32:50,850 --> 00:32:54,010
Why do some customers drop off after the trial phase?

623
00:32:54,010 --> 00:32:56,290
Why do some others upgrade quickly?

624
00:32:56,290 --> 00:32:59,090
These patterns hold the key to our future strategy.

625
00:32:59,090 --> 00:33:00,090
Right.

626
00:33:00,090 --> 00:33:04,630
Maybe we should run more surveys, customer interviews, and even social listening.

627
00:33:04,630 --> 00:33:06,410
People say a lot online.

628
00:33:06,410 --> 00:33:09,630
Complaints, praise, confusion, expectations.

629
00:33:09,630 --> 00:33:14,470
If we analyze these conversations, we'll understand how our brand fits into the broader

630
00:33:14,470 --> 00:33:15,470
market.

631
00:33:15,470 --> 00:33:19,030
Once we know how people talk about us, we'll know how to talk to them.

632
00:33:19,030 --> 00:33:20,030
Yes.

633
00:33:20,030 --> 00:33:23,790
And while we're on the topic of feedback, let's discuss user-generated content.

634
00:33:23,790 --> 00:33:26,310
We're not leveraging it nearly enough.

635
00:33:26,310 --> 00:33:30,750
Customers love authenticity, and nothing is more authentic than real users showing

636
00:33:30,750 --> 00:33:32,350
real experiences.

637
00:33:32,350 --> 00:33:34,150
It builds trust instantly.

638
00:33:34,150 --> 00:33:35,510
I agree.

639
00:33:35,510 --> 00:33:44,230
And UGC is cheaper, more organic, and more persuasive than Paula.

640
00:33:44,230 --> 00:33:45,790
Good morning.

641
00:33:45,790 --> 00:33:47,310
Thanks for meeting with me today.

642
00:33:47,310 --> 00:33:53,310
I know your schedule has been absolutely overloaded, especially with all the moving parts around

643
00:33:53,310 --> 00:33:59,470
the upcoming rebranding discussions, but I think this conversation is overdue.

644
00:33:59,470 --> 00:34:05,570
We've been circling around the same issues for weeks, mentioning ideas and passing,

645
00:34:05,570 --> 00:34:09,830
But we haven't had a focused, comprehensive dialogue.

646
00:34:09,830 --> 00:34:15,650
And given the pressure we're under, from leadership, from competitors, and from customers, we really

647
00:34:15,650 --> 00:34:17,550
need clarity.

648
00:34:17,550 --> 00:34:19,670
So let's jump into it.

649
00:34:19,670 --> 00:34:22,910
How are you feeling about where things stand?

650
00:34:22,910 --> 00:34:23,910
Good morning.

651
00:34:23,910 --> 00:34:25,010
I agree.

652
00:34:25,010 --> 00:34:27,800
We really needed this meeting.

653
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:33,000
To be honest, I'd been feeling a bit conflicted about the brand direction.

654
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:38,000
in a negative way, but there's this sense that our identity is shifting faster than

655
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:43,920
our messaging, and our messaging is shifting faster than our actual customer perception.

656
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:50,080
There's a gap forming, and if we don't align soon, the market is going to define our brand

657
00:34:50,080 --> 00:34:53,780
for us rather than us defining it.

658
00:34:53,780 --> 00:34:55,780
That's never a good place to be.

659
00:34:55,780 --> 00:35:01,020
So yes, I'm glad we're talking about positioning and what the rebrand should truly reflect.

660
00:35:01,020 --> 00:35:04,380
Exactly, and that's the core of the issue.

661
00:35:04,380 --> 00:35:07,300
The brand can't just look better.

662
00:35:07,300 --> 00:35:08,960
It has to mean something.

663
00:35:08,960 --> 00:35:14,140
It has to be aligned with who we are, where we're going, and what our customers expect

664
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:15,780
from us.

665
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:21,060
Over the past few months, I've had multiple conversations with customers, sales teams,

666
00:35:21,060 --> 00:35:25,680
and even external partners, and I'm noticing the same pattern.

667
00:35:25,680 --> 00:35:32,160
People know what we do, but they don't necessarily know what we stand for.

668
00:35:32,160 --> 00:35:36,600
That's a risky position for any business, especially in a crowded market.

669
00:35:36,600 --> 00:35:39,120
I've been hearing the same thing.

670
00:35:39,120 --> 00:35:43,760
People recognize our service offering, they're aware of our products, and they trust our

671
00:35:43,760 --> 00:35:48,340
capabilities, but emotionally, the brand doesn't connect.

672
00:35:48,340 --> 00:35:54,760
It's functional, rational, and transactional, but brands that grow, brands that scale

673
00:35:54,760 --> 00:35:57,880
are brands that build emotional resonance.

674
00:35:57,880 --> 00:36:00,000
It's not enough for customers to think

675
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:01,880
they deliver good services.

676
00:36:01,880 --> 00:36:05,000
They have to think, this brand aligns with my values.

677
00:36:05,000 --> 00:36:06,760
This brand understands me.

678
00:36:06,760 --> 00:36:09,040
And currently, I don't think we're there.

679
00:36:09,040 --> 00:36:10,360
No, we're not.

680
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:14,200
And what surprises me is that our culture internally

681
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:17,120
is much stronger than our external identity.

682
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:20,320
Employees feel proud, they feel connected,

683
00:36:20,320 --> 00:36:22,440
they collaborate well.

684
00:36:22,440 --> 00:36:24,480
But that sense of purpose and energy

685
00:36:24,480 --> 00:36:30,640
doesn't translate outwards. If anything, our external brand materials feel dated. The visuals

686
00:36:30,640 --> 00:36:37,120
are old-fashioned, the messaging is vague, the tone of voice feels corporate, not human.

687
00:36:37,120 --> 00:36:41,760
And in a market that values authenticity, that's not going to work anymore.

688
00:36:42,400 --> 00:36:49,840
Absolutely. Our tone is polite, professional, but flat. It doesn't inspire. It doesn't

689
00:36:49,840 --> 00:36:54,300
It doesn't challenge, it doesn't differentiate, and differentiation is what positioning is

690
00:36:54,300 --> 00:36:56,180
all about.

691
00:36:56,180 --> 00:36:58,820
That brings me to something I wanted to ask you.

692
00:36:58,820 --> 00:37:03,740
In your view, who do you think we really are as a brand?

693
00:37:03,740 --> 00:37:09,280
Not the standard mission statement, not the corporate jargon, but the authentic identity

694
00:37:09,280 --> 00:37:10,280
at the core.

695
00:37:10,280 --> 00:37:12,980
That's a good question.

696
00:37:12,980 --> 00:37:18,100
I think at our core, we're a brand that stands for progress.

697
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:22,420
We help customers improve, grow, adapt, and succeed.

698
00:37:22,420 --> 00:37:27,820
Whether it's through our technology or our services, we're essentially an enabler of

699
00:37:27,820 --> 00:37:29,420
transformation.

700
00:37:29,420 --> 00:37:32,220
But we never articulate that clearly.

701
00:37:32,220 --> 00:37:36,420
Instead we talk about features, pricing, and performance metrics.

702
00:37:36,420 --> 00:37:42,660
Meanwhile, our competitors talk about vision, innovation, empowerment, and identity.

703
00:37:42,660 --> 00:37:47,020
They're owning the emotional space while we're stuck in the rational lane.

704
00:37:47,020 --> 00:37:49,220
That's exactly how I see it, too.

705
00:37:49,220 --> 00:37:50,860
And the funny thing is, you're right.

706
00:37:50,860 --> 00:37:53,780
Our internal culture embodies transformation.

707
00:37:53,780 --> 00:37:57,700
We're always innovating, always improving, always moving.

708
00:37:57,700 --> 00:38:00,980
But we communicate like a conservative, slow-paced,

709
00:38:00,980 --> 00:38:02,580
risk-averse brand.

710
00:38:02,580 --> 00:38:05,260
The gap between who we are and who we sound like

711
00:38:05,260 --> 00:38:06,540
is getting bigger.

712
00:38:06,540 --> 00:38:10,020
And that gap is what the rebranding must solve.

713
00:38:10,020 --> 00:38:11,580
But here's the challenge.

714
00:38:11,580 --> 00:38:15,100
Rebranding cannot just be about changing logos and colors.

715
00:38:15,100 --> 00:38:17,100
It has to start with positioning.

716
00:38:17,100 --> 00:38:21,600
Yes. Positioning first, identity second.

717
00:38:21,600 --> 00:38:26,100
And positioning must answer simple but powerful questions.

718
00:38:26,100 --> 00:38:28,100
What makes us different?

719
00:38:28,100 --> 00:38:30,100
Why do customers choose us?

720
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:33,100
Why should they choose us in the future?

721
00:38:33,100 --> 00:38:37,100
What role do we play in their lives or businesses?

722
00:38:37,100 --> 00:38:41,600
What value do we deliver that no one else can?

723
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:46,600
And I think we haven't clearly defined those answers in years.

724
00:38:46,600 --> 00:38:50,100
Honestly, I think we've never defined them properly.

725
00:38:50,100 --> 00:38:52,600
We've always had broad statements like,

726
00:38:52,600 --> 00:38:57,600
we deliver quality, or we focus on customer satisfaction.

727
00:38:57,600 --> 00:38:59,600
But those aren't differentiators.

728
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:03,140
They're expectations. They're table stakes.

729
00:39:03,140 --> 00:39:06,640
If we want a strong position, we need something bold.

730
00:39:06,640 --> 00:39:08,640
Something meaningful.

731
00:39:08,640 --> 00:39:13,520
something that makes a customer instantly understand not only the function of what we offer?
